<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/16th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Count's Feud</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1534–1536</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Grevens Fejde</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Count's Feud was a Danish civil war of 1534 to 1536, named after Count Christopher of Oldenburg, who led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a>'s forces in the name of the deposed King Christian II. Lübeck under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurgen-wullenwever">Jürgen Wullenwever</a>, allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a>, Malmö and rebellious peasants, opposed the Lutheran Duke Christian of Holstein. Christian's victory made him King Christian III, led to the Lutheran <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> and ended Lübeck's dominance in the Baltic.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/counts-feud.jpg" alt="Count's Feud" width="320" height="310" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hermann Hamelmann · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Koebenhavn1536.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
